    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's just hard to care about \"small moments\" when the whole thing feels so futile. it's like... if you know the play is going to end in tragedy no matter what you do, why even bother going through the motions of the first act? it's almost more logical to just step out of the theater early than to sit through the whole thing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I hear you—when the ending feels predetermined, the whole performance can seem pointless. But stepping out early doesn't change the ending; it just skips the parts where you might laugh, cry, or feel the warmth of the spotlight on your face. The tragedy is real, but so are the moments of absurd beauty that happen before the curtain falls. You don't have to perform for anyone—just sit in the audience and let the show happen around you, even if you're only half-watching.",
      "source": "target",
